==English==

===Etymology===
{{af|en|demono-|-cracy}}.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n}}</ref> The second sense is a pun on {{m|en|democracy}}. First use appears c. 1713.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɒkɹəsi|s=5}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[government]] by or of (supernatural) [[demon]]s, such as the government which in some mythologies exists in [[Hell]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1717|title=An English Dictionary - Explaining the Difficult Terms|author=Elisha Coles|passage='''Demonocracy''', the Government of Devils ...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1848|author=w:Frances Wright|title=England, The Civilizer: Her History Developed in its Principles| passage=Thus we see, that while the theological and doctrinal poet inaugurated male supremacy in the person of the godhead in heaven, of humanity on earth, and of '''demonocracy''' in hell, so did the philosopher propound the universal prevalence, and irresistable omnipotence, of the male principle throughout the whole of existence.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1904|author=Julius Goebel|title=The Etymology of Mephistopheles|journal=Transactions and Proceedings of the American Philological Association|volume=35|page=148
|passage={{...}} a magic book printed in 1612, in which the various dignitaries of the '''demonocracy''' are described according to their rank and position.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|title=The Gestapo on Trial - Evidence from Nuremberg|author=Bob Carruthers (editor)|page=303|passage=The effect of '''demonocracy''' in the wide world has become clear to you in some of the cases of the individual defendants.}}
# A [[democracy]]. {{qualifier|when regarded as resulting in, or being no better than, a government run by figurative demons (i.e., malevolent people), or when otherwise held in contempt}}.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author=Sidney J. Slomich| title=The American Nightmare| page=206| passage=Tuning in, turning on, and dropping out are the politics of '''demonocracy''', the counsel of despair leaving the field to adversaries, {{...}} }}
#* '''2005-06''', {{w|Matthew Bellamy}}, "{{w|Assassin (Muse song)|Assassin}}", ''{{w|Black Holes and Revelations}}''
#*: Oppose and disagree, destroy '''demonocracy'''.

